{
  "chapter": "first-agent/the-loop-by-hand",
  "tier": "build",
  "notes": [
    "Atlas v0. An agent is a bounded loop around a stateless function: the structure is",
    "trivial and the boundary conditions are the engineering.",
    "Four exits. `answered` and `escalated` belong to the model; `halted` belongs to you, and",
    "it carries the bound that stopped the run.",
    "Bounds are checked BEFORE the call, never after. A budget enforced after the spend is a",
    "report, not a limit — which is why a halted run can finish over its cap and still never",
    "have started the step that would have blown past it.",
    "Halting is a first-class outcome. Falling out of the bottom of the loop and returning",
    "whatever text was lying around is the bug this whole shape exists to prevent.",
    "escalate_to_human is terminal. It ends the turn it was requested in, and no other tool in",
    "that same turn runs.",
    "Authorization is an argument the tool enforces, never an instruction in the prompt. The",
    "model chooses the arguments, so the customerId filter lives in the tool.",
    "`steps` is the number of model calls actually made, and `messages` is how long the",
    "history grew. Every step resends all of it."
  ],
